# {{DisplayName}}

A plugin for __APP_TITLE__, built against host SDK v{{HostSdk}}.

## Build

```sh
dotnet build {{AssemblyName}}/{{AssemblyName}}.csproj -p:Platform=x64
```

The project references the application's assemblies from the directory it was scaffolded against, so
it compiles against exactly the ones it will be loaded beside. `Private=false` on every one of them:
the host already has these, and a copy in the package would be dead weight — the load context
resolves by simple name, and the host's is already loaded.

Two of them, deliberately. The application's shared controls — its page header, its cards — are
available at runtime, but referring to them by path brings their whole package graph into this
project to restore. A starter project uses the framework's own controls; add the reference and its
packages when you want the shared ones.

## What is where

| | |
| --- | --- |
| `manifest.json` | what the package declares, and the only thing read before any of its code runs |
| `{{AssemblyName}}/{{Module}}Module.cs` | the entry point the host activates |
| `{{AssemblyName}}/Views/` | pages, whose compiled XAML goes into this assembly's own resource index |

`DisableEmbeddedXbf` is set to `false` in the project file. That is what puts the compiled XAML in
the resource index the host loads; without it the pages build and then cannot be found at runtime.

## Rules the host enforces at load

- A page type's name ends in `Page`, and its key — the name without that suffix — may not be one the
  application already answers to.
- `RegisterServices` is additive. A registration that removes or replaces something the host
  registered is rolled back and the plugin does not load.
- The manifest's `minHostSdk` is checked before the assembly is opened, so a package built against a
  later host is refused with a sentence rather than failing inside XAML.

## Pack it

```sh
dotnet tool install --global __APP_NAME__.PluginTool
__APP_NAME_LOWER__-plugin validate .
__APP_NAME_LOWER__-plugin pack .
```

`validate` packs the project in memory and checks the archive, so what it approves is the file
`pack` would write rather than a directory that resembles one. It reports what the manifest gets
wrong, a `moduleType` the entry assembly does not declare, a page whose name would break its key, a
resource index that is not declared, a screen the manifest promises and the package cannot show,
and `x:Uid` in markup — a plugin's own `.resw` strings do not resolve, so a localized label renders
empty. The Plugins screen's Develop tab runs the same checks out of the same library.

What it does not do is run your module, so it cannot confirm that `GetNavigationItems()` agrees with
the manifest, and it sees only pages that derive directly from `Page`.

## Install it

Install the `.plugin` from the application's Plugins screen. It loads at the next launch: a plugin's
services are registered while the container is still open, which is earlier than any screen exists.
